WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.836 --> 00:00:12.050
In February 2012, pro skier Elyse Saugstad
was carving through the slopes

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:13.676
of the Cascade Mountains

00:00:13.676 --> 00:00:16.304
when a crack formed
in the snowpack above her.

00:00:16.596 --> 00:00:20.683
Before she was fully engulfed
by a 5,000-ton wall of snow,

00:00:20.808 --> 00:00:26.022
Elyse heard “AVALANCHE!” just in time
to react with a lifesaving maneuver.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.068
So, how could she survive
this crushing snow surge?

00:00:30.777 --> 00:00:36.365
Avalanches are the rapid descension of
snow, ice, rock, or a combination of these

00:00:36.365 --> 00:00:37.450
down a slope.

00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:41.287
They can be destructive natural disasters
and personal tragedies:

00:00:41.454 --> 00:00:47.210
in the United States alone, avalanches
kill an average of 27 people annually.

00:00:47.627 --> 00:00:50.797
These alpine nightmares
don’t just awaken out of nowhere;

00:00:51.047 --> 00:00:53.841
they need three specific conditions.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.553
First, the mountain needs
to have the right slope,

00:00:58.137 --> 00:01:01.224
typically between 30 and 45 degrees.

00:01:01.557 --> 00:01:05.061
This is shallow enough
to allow snow to build up on the slope,

00:01:05.228 --> 00:01:09.482
yet steep enough to provide enough
potential energy for movement.

00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:13.528
Second, they typically need
a layer of sturdy snow

00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:16.322
deposited over a weak or unstable layer,

00:01:16.489 --> 00:01:19.617
or what’s sometimes called
“something over nothing.”

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:21.994
Take the Cascade Mountain avalanche,

00:01:21.994 --> 00:01:27.792
where recent heavy storms deposited a
nearly 1-meter-deep slab of fresh snow.

00:01:28.292 --> 00:01:31.254
Below this sat a thin layer
of surface hoar.

00:01:31.337 --> 00:01:33.840
These are delicate, feathery ice crystals

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.386
that form when water vapor in the air
rapidly freezes on the surface of snow,

00:01:38.553 --> 00:01:40.721
almost like dew in the wintertime.

00:01:41.305 --> 00:01:43.641
Finally, avalanches need a trigger,

00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:47.603
something to collapse the weak layer
and put the snow in motion.

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:52.984
A trigger can be natural, such
as heavy snowfall, strong winds, rainfall,

00:01:52.984 --> 00:01:55.027
or even other avalanches.

00:01:55.444 --> 00:02:00.533
Or they can be human-caused, like the
surface impact of skiers or snowmobilers.

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:04.036
Since fractures can propagate
quickly through the snowpack,

00:02:04.036 --> 00:02:06.831
triggers can originate
hundreds of meters away—

00:02:06.831 --> 00:02:11.586
upstream, downstream, or even adjacent
to the avalanche’s path.

00:02:12.086 --> 00:02:17.258
Once in motion, how quickly an avalanche
moves depends on the slope’s steepness,

00:02:17.466 --> 00:02:21.345
its exposure or vegetation cover,
terrain roughness,

00:02:21.345 --> 00:02:24.265
and the type and amount of snow involved.

00:02:24.765 --> 00:02:30.313
For example, dry, light, and loosely
bonded snow tends to travel fast,

00:02:30.313 --> 00:02:32.648
forming a powder cloud as it moves.

00:02:33.065 --> 00:02:35.902
In contrast, wet snow avalanches,

00:02:35.902 --> 00:02:38.237
caused by water moving
through the snowpack,

00:02:38.237 --> 00:02:42.700
travel more slowly but are much denser
and can be particularly destructive.

00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:47.830
Larger avalanches can travel
more than 160 kilometers per hour,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.126
breaking trees, destroying buildings,
and burying roadways.

00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:56.672
In 1970, one particularly
massive avalanche

00:02:56.756 --> 00:03:00.218
consisting of 50 to 100 million
cubic meters

00:03:00.218 --> 00:03:03.596
of glacial ice, snow, mud, and rock

00:03:03.763 --> 00:03:07.558
reached speeds of over
300 kilometers per hour.

00:03:07.892 --> 00:03:10.728
This avalanche proved
to be the deadliest in history,

00:03:10.728 --> 00:03:15.650
engulfing an entire Peruvian town
and killing 18,000 people.

00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:18.778
Avalanches aren't always this large,

00:03:18.903 --> 00:03:22.823
but even smaller ones can be dangerous
for anyone caught in their path.

00:03:22.949 --> 00:03:27.703
As snow rushes downhill, it can behave
like both a solid and a liquid,

00:03:27.703 --> 00:03:30.206
making its movement difficult to predict.

00:03:30.581 --> 00:03:32.208
At the fastest-moving front,

00:03:32.208 --> 00:03:35.419
surface snow moves faster
than the snow beneath,

00:03:35.461 --> 00:03:40.049
pulling victims deep into the snowpack
and scattering them across wide areas,

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:41.634
making rescue difficult.

00:03:42.301 --> 00:03:47.556
Once an avalanche slows, the snow loses
its fluid-like properties and compacts,

00:03:47.848 --> 00:03:51.727
leaving victims immobilized and
unable to escape on their own.

00:03:52.228 --> 00:03:55.398
For these reasons, the best
way to survive an avalanche

00:03:55.398 --> 00:03:57.233
is to avoid one altogether.

00:03:57.942 --> 00:04:02.780
Thankfully, at most ski resorts,
parks, and even highways,

00:04:03.155 --> 00:04:07.076
avalanche workers mitigate risk
by continuously monitoring the weather,

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:09.912
stability of the snowpack,
and human activity.

00:04:10.037 --> 00:04:11.622
If a threat is detected,

00:04:11.622 --> 00:04:12.915
they'll close terrain

00:04:12.957 --> 00:04:16.085
and may even intentionally trigger
a controlled avalanche

00:04:16.085 --> 00:04:17.962
with explosives or artillery.

00:04:18.546 --> 00:04:23.467
However, there are regions where
avalanche risk is not actively mitigated,

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.219
known as the backcountry.

00:04:25.553 --> 00:04:28.889
Here, you are responsible
for managing your own risk.

00:04:29.098 --> 00:04:32.143
Local avalanche centers
provide avalanche forecasts,

00:04:32.143 --> 00:04:35.146
and anyone entering the backcountry
should understand them,

00:04:35.146 --> 00:04:38.691
be properly trained,
and carry essential rescue gear.

00:04:38.941 --> 00:04:41.485
And ultimately, in high-hazard terrain,

00:04:41.485 --> 00:04:44.280
there's no way to completely
eliminate risk.

00:04:44.697 --> 00:04:49.076
The group at the Cascade Mountains
consisted of highly experienced skiers.

00:04:49.285 --> 00:04:53.080
Yet the area was very exposed,
and following a large storm,

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.750
the avalanche danger forecast
was elevated.

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:59.128
In the end, three skiers lost their lives.

00:04:59.295 --> 00:05:03.257
But not Elyse, as she was able
to deploy an inflatable airbag.

00:05:03.591 --> 00:05:07.386
These devices are imperfect
and a last line of defense,

00:05:07.386 --> 00:05:09.972
yet can keep a victim closer
to the surface,

00:05:09.972 --> 00:05:13.476
improving their chance of rescue
should disaster strike.

